"{\"eval_set_result_id\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640842.8518312\",\"eval_set_result_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640842.8518312\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_case_results\":[{\"eval_set_file\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_id\":\"test-1\",\"final_eval_status\":2,\"eval_metric_results\":null,\"overall_eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":1.0,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":1.0,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.5664880662445202,\"eval_status\":2,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}],\"eval_metric_result_per_invocation\":[{\"actual_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-bc424be7-25db-49d9-a3d3-9e4d18eb07bc\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"**Agent Name:** TravelOptimizerAgent\\n\\nITINERARY: ## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive: Kyoto Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on maximizing your cultural immersion, balancing iconic sightseeing with authentic traditional experiences, structured geographically to minimize travel time.\\n\\n**Agent Name:** ItineraryBuilderAgent\\n\\n### Trip Overview\\n\\n*   **Duration:** 7 Days / 6 Nights\\n*   **Focus:** Temples, Shrines, Zen Gardens, Traditional Arts (Tea Ceremony), and Nature.\\n*   **Best Travel Style:** Public Transport (Bus/Subway) supplemented by walking. An ICOCA card is highly recommended.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\n**Gion/Higashiyama District:** This area is ideal for a cultural deep-dive. It keeps you close to historic streets (Ninenzaka, Sannenzaka), traditional restaurants, and many major eastern temples. It allows for early morning or late evening strolls in atmospheric neighborhoods.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Daily Itinerary Breakdown\\n\\n#### Day 1: Arrival and Eastern Gateway (Higashiyama South)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrive at KIX/ITM, take Haruka Express/Limo bus to Kyoto Station. Check into accommodation. | Logistics & Settling In | 3.5 hrs | Quick lunch near your hotel or Kyoto Station. |\\n| **14:00** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** (UNESCO Site) | Iconic views, wooden stage. (Visit slightly later to avoid peak crowds) | 2.0 hrs | |\\n| **16:30** | Walk down **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** Streets | Historic preserved streets, traditional shops. | 1.5 hrs | Try *Yatsuhashi* (sweet confection) samples. |\\n| **18:00** | **Early Evening Tea Ceremony:** Book a short, introductory ceremony in the Higashiyama area. | Tradition/Etiquette | 1.0 hr | |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner in Pontocho Alley | Atmospheric narrow alley known for dining. Try *Kushikatsu* (deep-fried skewers). | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a53,500\u2013\u00a56,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 1 Budget Estimate:** Local Transport (Train/Bus to hotel): \u00a53,500. Temple Entry: \u00a5400. Tea Ceremony: \u00a53,000. Dinner: \u00a54,500. **Total: ~\u00a511,400**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 2: Thousand Gates and Zen Serenity (South Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **07:30** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** Hike | Iconic *Torii* gates. Go early to avoid severe crowds and heat/sun. | 3.0 hrs | **Breakfast:** Grab snacks (*Inari sushi* or rice balls) from a convenience store near the shrine entrance. |\\n| **11:00** | Travel to Tofuku-ji Area | Transition via JR Nara Line or Keihan Line. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:30** | **Tofuku-ji Temple** | Explore the large Sanmon gate and its expansive, famous Zen gardens (especially Hojo Garden). | 2.0 hrs | **Lunch:** Near Tofuku-ji Station. Look for a small restaurant serving *udon* or *soba*. |\\n| **14:00** | Travel to Central Kyoto/Nijo Area | Take a taxi or bus/subway combination. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **15:00** | **Nijo Castle** | Historical exploration of the Shogun\u2019s residence, famous \\\"nightingale floors.\\\" | 2.0 hrs | |\\n| **18:30** | Dinner near Kyoto Station or back in Gion | Explore *Kyoto Station Cube* for diverse dining options, or find traditional *Izakaya* fare. | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a54,000\u2013\u00a57,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 2 Budget Estimate:** Transport: \u00a51,500. Temple Entries: \u00a51,000 (Tofuku-ji + Nijo Castle). Lunch/Dinner: \u00a56,500. **Total: ~\u00a59,000**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 3: Bamboo Forests and Western Majesty (Arashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **08:00** | Travel to Arashiyama (via JR Sagano Line) | Early start is crucial for quiet contemplation. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **08:30** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | Walk through the towering bamboo forest. | 1.0 hr | |\\n| **09:30** | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** (UNESCO Site) | Explore the main temple and its spectacular landscape garden. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:30** | **Okochi Sanso Villa** (Optional but recommended) | Former actor\u2019s villa with stunning views and immaculate gardens (includes tea). | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **13:30** | Lunch in Arashiyama Village | Look for restaurants specializing in *Yudofu* (simmered tofu), an Arashiyama specialty. | 1.5 hrs | **Lunch:** \u00a52,500\u2013\u00a54,000 pp |\\n| **15:30** | Togetsukyo Bridge & Tenryu-ji Temple Garden stroll. | Gentle nature walk. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **18:30** | Return to Central Kyoto & Dinner | Relaxed dinner near the hotel. Try Okonomiyaki (savory pancake). | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a53,000\u2013\u00a55,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 3 Budget Estimate:** Transport (Round Trip): \u00a51,000. Entry Fees: \u00a51,600 (Tenryu-ji + Villa). Meals: \u00a57,000. **Total: ~\u00a59,600**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 4: Golden Splendor and Zen Contemplation (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:00** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | Arrive shortly after opening for the best light on the gold leaf. (Bus recommended). | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **10:30** | Travel to Ryoan-ji | Short bus ride or 20-min walk. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:00** | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | Contemplate the famous, enigmatic rock garden. | 1.0 hr | |\\n| **12:30** | Lunch near Ryoan-ji/Kitano | Look for local, simple lunch spots frequented by locals. | 1.0 hr | **Lunch:** \u00a51,500\u2013\u00a52,500 pp |\\n| **14:00** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | Explore several sub-temples and quiet moss gardens; a deep dive into Zen architecture. | 2.5 hrs | |\\n| **17:00** | **Tea Experience near Daitoku-ji:** Look for a smaller, local tea house outside the main complex. | Deeper immersion into tea culture. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner: Nishiki Market Area | Explore the area surrounding Kyoto's kitchen for fresh seafood or specialty dishes. | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a55,000\u2013\u00a58,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 4 Budget Estimate:** Transport (Bus Day Pass recommended): \u00a5700. Temple Entries: \u00a51,100. Tea Experience: \u00a53,500. Meals: \u00a57,000. **Total: ~\u00a512,300**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 5: Reflection and Philosophy (Higashiyama North)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:30** | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | Explore the moss gardens and dry sand gardens surrounding the pavilion. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:00** | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)** | Leisurely walk along the canal, stopping at small shrines along the way. | 2.0 hrs | **Snack:** Stop at a small cafe along the path for coffee or matcha ice cream. |\\n| **13:30** | Lunch in the Ginkaku-ji/Path area | Look for quieter, more refined lunch options. | 1.5 hrs | **Lunch:** \u00a52,000\u2013\u00a53,500 pp |\\n| **15:30** | **Formal Tea Ceremony (*Chaji* Intensive)** | Book a longer, comprehensive session (2-3 hours) to learn the full ritual and etiquette. | 3.0 hrs | Pre-book this well in advance. |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner & Gion Exploration | Casual dinner, then an evening stroll through Gion hoping for a glimpse of a *Geiko* or *Maiko*. (Remember etiquette!) | 2.5 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a54,000\u2013\u00a56,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 5 Budget Estimate:** Transport: \u00a51,000. Entry Fees: \u00a5500. Intensive Tea Ceremony: \u00a57,000\u2013\u00a510,000. Meals: \u00a56,500. **Total: ~\u00a515,000\u2013\u00a518,000** (Higher due to intensive tea class)\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 6: Hidden Gems and Artisanal Crafts\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:30** | **Heian Jingu Shrine** | Visit the massive *torii* gate and explore the impressive, colorful gardens behind the main hall. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:30** | **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design** (or Kyoto Museum of Traditional Crafts) | See demonstrations of weaving, dyeing, and pottery that maintain Kyoto\u2019s heritage. | 2.0 hrs | |\\n| **14:00** | Lunch in Downtown Kyoto (Kawaramachi/Shijo area) | Experience a modern aspect of Kyoto cuisine. | 1.5 hrs | **Lunch:** Try Kyoto-style ramen or *Gyukatsu*. |\\n| **16:00** | **Nishiki Market (Return Visit)** | Focus on tasting different local pickles (*tsukemono*), sweets, and street food for late afternoon nibbles. | 2.0 hrs | **Snack Focused** |\\n| **19:30** | Farewell Dinner: *Kaiseki* Experience | Splurge on a traditional multi-course *Kaiseki* meal (pre-booking essential). | 2.5 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a510,000+ pp |\\n\\n**Day 6 Budget Estimate:** Transport: \u00a51,000. Entry Fees: \u00a5600. Meals/Tasting: \u00a513,000 (including Kaiseki). **Total: ~\u00a514,600**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 7: Mountain Spirituality and Departure (Optional Hike/Departure)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Option A (Nature Hike)** | **Kurama-dera Temple & Kifune Shrine** | Take the Eizan Electric Railway north. Hike between the two mountain temples for a spiritual forest experience. (Requires 5-6 hours round trip plus travel). | Full Day | Lunch near Kifune Shrine (known for *Nagashi Somen* if in season). |\\n| **Option B (Relaxed Departure)** | Revisit a favorite spot (e.g., Ryoan-ji for quiet reflection) or visit **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** (1001 statues of Kannon). | Final sightseeing/Souvenir shopping. | 3.0 hrs | **Lunch:** Light lunch near Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Head to Kyoto Station, board transport for KIX/ITM or next destination. | Departure Logistics | 2.5 hrs | |\\n\\n**Day 7 Budget Estimate (Option B):** Transport: \u00a51,500. Entry: \u00a5800. Lunch: \u00a52,500. **Total: ~\u00a54,800 + Airport transfer**\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Budget Summary (Estimated Major Expenses)\\n\\nThis estimation excludes international flights but covers on-the-ground costs for one person based on the itinerary above, assuming mid-range dining choices (with one splurge meal).\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Average | Total 7-Day Estimate | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** | \u00a515,000 | \u00a590,000 (~$600 USD) | Based on mid-range hotel or high-quality Airbnb/Ryokan. |\\n| **Local Transport** | \u00a51,000 | \u00a57,000 | Covers buses, subways, and a few short taxi rides/train segments. |\\n| **Entry Fees/Activities** | \u00a51,500 | \u00a510,500 | Includes temple fees and one major specialized activity (Kaiseki excluded). |\\n| **Meals & Drinks** | \u00a59,000 | \u00a563,000 | Includes 6 dinners, 6 lunches, and daily snacks/drinks. |\\n| **Special Activities** | N/A | \u00a515,000 | Covers the intensive Tea Ceremony/Masterclass cost difference. |\\n| **Splurge Dinner (Kaiseki)** | N/A | \u00a512,000 | Allocated specifically for Day 6 splurge. |\\n| **Buffer/Souvenirs** | N/A | \u00a520,000 | Recommended for unforeseen purchases or emergencies. |\\n| **ESTIMATED TOTAL** | **~\u00a527,000/day** | **\u00a5217,500 (~$1,450 USD)** | *Excludes flights to/from Japan.* |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### OPTIMIZATION TIPS: Money-Saving & Budget Alternatives\\n\\n1.  **ICOCA Card Usage:** While essential, remember that the ICOCA card sometimes has minor automatic rounding or transaction fees when topping up. If you are *only* using buses for an entire day (Day 4, 5), purchasing the **Kyoto Bus One-Day Pass (\u00a5700)** is almost always cheaper than three separate rides (\u00a5230 x 3 = \u00a5690 base fare, but the pass is cleaner).\\n2.  **Temple Entry Savings:** Many temples charge entrance fees ranging from \u00a5400\u2013\u00a5600. To save significantly, prioritize **free major sites** like the main grounds of Fushimi Inari Taisha (hiking trail) and Heian Jingu (main shrine/torii). Focus entrance fees only on the Zen gardens (Ryoan-ji, Tofuku-ji).\\n3.  **Dining on a Dime:**\\n    *   **Budget Breakfasts:** Utilize Japanese convenience stores (*Konbini* like 7-Eleven, FamilyMart) for excellent, cheap breakfast staples (onigiri, bread, coffee) for under \u00a5600.\\n    *   **Lunch Sets (*Teishoku*):** Instead of expensive dinners, seek out *Teishoku* (set meals) offered during lunch hours (11:30 AM \u2013 2:00 PM). These often include miso soup, rice, and a main dish for \u00a51,200\u2013\u00a51,800.\\n    *   **Nishiki Market Strategy (Day 6):** Make Nishiki Market your main lunch/afternoon meal on Day 6. Grazing on high-quality street food snacks is often cheaper than a formal sit-down lunch.\\n4.  **Accommodation Location:** Staying in Higashiyama/Gion is atmospheric but pricier. **Budget Alternative:** Look at areas slightly further out but on a subway line, such as **Karasuma-Oike** or near **Kyoto Station**. You save on nightly rates and use the subway (faster than buses) to reach your sites.\\n\\n### TRAVEL ESSENTIALS: Packing & Preparation Advice\\n\\n1.  **Footwear is Critical:** You will be walking miles daily, often on uneven cobblestones (Sannenzaka) or stone steps (Fushimi Inari). Pack 2-3 pairs of *broken-in* walking shoes. Comfort > Style.\\n2.  **Layering for Temples:** Wear easily removable footwear and socks. Since you remove shoes frequently, wear clean, hole-free socks. Layered clothing is vital, as interior temples can be cool even if the outside is warm.\\n3.  **Temple Kit:** Carry a small, light scarf or hand towel. This is useful for drying hands after using the *temizuya* purification fountain or wiping sweat.\\n4.  **Weather Contingency:** If traveling during the rainy season (June/July) or shoulder seasons, pack a lightweight, packable rain jacket and a compact umbrella.\\n5.  **SIM/Connectivity:** A pocket Wi-Fi router or a local eSIM (e.g., Sakura Mobile, Ubigi) is highly recommended for real-time map navigation, especially since temple layouts can be confusing.\\n\\n### BACKUP PLANS: Weather & Unexpected Situations\\n\\n1.  **Rainy Day Plan (Applicable to Days 3, 4, or 5):**\\n    *   **Arashiyama Backup (Day 3):** If the weather is terrible, skip the nature walk, and instead spend more time inside the **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple Museum** or explore the **Arashiyama Monkey Park Iwatayama** (if rain is light).\\n    *   **Northern Kyoto Backup (Day 4):** If rain is heavy, focus more time inside the large, covered areas of **Daitoku-ji** complex, or substitute Kinkaku-ji with the **Kyoto Imperial Palace** (requires prior booking, but is indoors).\\n2.  **Crowd Overload Backup:** If Fushimi Inari or Bamboo Grove are overwhelmingly crowded (even early morning):\\n    *   **Fushimi Inari Alternative:** Hike higher up the mountain path. The crowds thin out significantly after the first 30 minutes.\\n    *   **Bamboo Grove Alternative:** Head immediately to **Okochi Sanso Villa** (Day 3); its gardens are beautifully managed and feel less congested than the main path.\\n3.  **Geiko/Maiko Encounter Etiquette (Day 5):** If you are lucky enough to see a *Geiko* or *Maiko*, **DO NOT** stop them, block their path, touch them, or chase them for photos. They are working professionals. Observe quietly from a distance. Photography is generally only acceptable if you are seated at an outdoor cafe and they pass by at a distance.\\n\\n### LOCAL APPS, WEBSITES, & RESOURCES\\n\\n1.  **Google Maps:** Essential for navigation. It provides highly accurate real-time bus routes, subway schedules, and walking directions, including platform numbers.\\n2.  **Japan Transit Planner (Jorudan) / Navitime:** Useful apps for checking longer-distance train routes (like KIX arrival) or confirming specific train schedules if you decide to opt for the Day 7 hike.\\n3.  **Google Translate:** Download the Japanese language pack for offline use. The camera function is excellent for translating menus and signage.\\n4.  **Kyoto City Bus Official Website:** Check their site (or maps) for any temporary route diversions or service alerts, which are common during peak seasons.\\n5.  **Booking Sites:** For specialized activities like the **Formal Tea Ceremony (*Chaji*) on Day 5**, use reliable services like **TableCheck** or **Voyagin/Klook** for secure, pre-paid bookings with English confirmation.\\n\\n### CULTURAL DO'S & DON'TS\\n\\n| DO (Respectful Travel) | DON'T (Avoid Offense) |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **DO** accept items (like business cards or souvenirs) with both hands. | **DON'T** eat, drink, or smoke while walking, especially in historic districts like Gion. |\\n| **DO** use the *temizuya* (purification fountain) correctly at shrines (left hand, right hand, rinse mouth, clean ladle). | **DON'T** step on the threshold (*shikii*) when entering a home or traditional building; step over it. |\\n| **DO** speak quietly inside temples, buses, and trains. Phone calls on public transport are strictly frowned upon. | **DON'T** point directly at people, especially *Geiko/Maiko*, or point with your chopsticks at food. |\\n| **DO** carry small change (\u00a5100/\u00a5500 coins) for small temple entry fees and vending machines. | **DON'T** leave tips anywhere; it can imply the service was inadequate. |\\n| **DO** buy *omamori* (charms) at shrines for luck if you connect with the location. | **DON'T** stick your chopsticks upright into your rice bowl (this mimics funeral rites). |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"invocation_events\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640818.69788,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":{\"agent_details\":{\"DestinationResearchAgent\":{\"name\":\"DestinationResearchAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a travel researcher. You will be given a destination and travel preferences, and you will research:\\n    - Best time to visit and weather patterns\\n    - Top attractions and must-see locations\\n    - Local culture, customs, and etiquette tips\\n    - Transportation options within the destination\\n    - Safety considerations and travel requirements\\n    Provide comprehensive destination insights for trip planning.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"DestinationResearchAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that researches travel destinations and gathers essential information\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[{\"function_declarations\":null,\"retrieval\":null,\"google_search_retrieval\":null,\"computer_use\":null,\"file_search\":null,\"code_execution\":null,\"enterprise_web_search\":null,\"google_maps\":null,\"google_search\":{\"exclude_domains\":null,\"blocking_confidence\":null,\"time_range_filter\":null},\"url_context\":null}]},\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\":{\"name\":\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a professional travel planner. Using the research from \\\"destination_research\\\" output, create a detailed itinerary that includes:\\n    - Day-by-day schedule with recommended activities\\n    - Suggested accommodation areas or districts\\n    - Estimated time requirements for each activity\\n    - Meal recommendations and dining suggestions\\n    - Budget estimates for major expenses\\n    Structure it logically for easy following during the trip.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that creates structured travel itineraries with daily schedules\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]},\"TravelOptimizerAgent\":{\"name\":\"TravelOptimizerAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a seasoned travel consultant. Using the itinerary from \\\"travel_itinerary\\\" output, optimize it by adding:\\n    - Money-saving tips and budget alternatives\\n    - Packing recommendations specific to the destination\\n    - Backup plans for weather or unexpected situations\\n    - Local apps, websites, or resources to download\\n    - Cultural do's and don'ts for respectful travel\\n\\n    Format the final output as:\\n    \\n    ITINERARY: ## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive: Kyoto Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on maximizing your cultural immersion, balancing iconic sightseeing with authentic traditional experiences, structured geographically to minimize travel time.\\n\\n**Agent Name:** ItineraryBuilderAgent\\n\\n### Trip Overview\\n\\n*   **Duration:** 7 Days / 6 Nights\\n*   **Focus:** Temples, Shrines, Zen Gardens, Traditional Arts (Tea Ceremony), and Nature.\\n*   **Best Travel Style:** Public Transport (Bus/Subway) supplemented by walking. An ICOCA card is highly recommended.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\n**Gion/Higashiyama District:** This area is ideal for a cultural deep-dive. It keeps you close to historic streets (Ninenzaka, Sannenzaka), traditional restaurants, and many major eastern temples. It allows for early morning or late evening strolls in atmospheric neighborhoods.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Daily Itinerary Breakdown\\n\\n#### Day 1: Arrival and Eastern Gateway (Higashiyama South)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrive at KIX/ITM, take Haruka Express/Limo bus to Kyoto Station. Check into accommodation. | Logistics & Settling In | 3.5 hrs | Quick lunch near your hotel or Kyoto Station. |\\n| **14:00** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** (UNESCO Site) | Iconic views, wooden stage. (Visit slightly later to avoid peak crowds) | 2.0 hrs | |\\n| **16:30** | Walk down **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** Streets | Historic preserved streets, traditional shops. | 1.5 hrs | Try *Yatsuhashi* (sweet confection) samples. |\\n| **18:00** | **Early Evening Tea Ceremony:** Book a short, introductory ceremony in the Higashiyama area. | Tradition/Etiquette | 1.0 hr | |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner in Pontocho Alley | Atmospheric narrow alley known for dining. Try *Kushikatsu* (deep-fried skewers). | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a53,500\u2013\u00a56,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 1 Budget Estimate:** Local Transport (Train/Bus to hotel): \u00a53,500. Temple Entry: \u00a5400. Tea Ceremony: \u00a53,000. Dinner: \u00a54,500. **Total: ~\u00a511,400**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 2: Thousand Gates and Zen Serenity (South Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **07:30** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** Hike | Iconic *Torii* gates. Go early to avoid severe crowds and heat/sun. | 3.0 hrs | **Breakfast:** Grab snacks (*Inari sushi* or rice balls) from a convenience store near the shrine entrance. |\\n| **11:00** | Travel to Tofuku-ji Area | Transition via JR Nara Line or Keihan Line. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:30** | **Tofuku-ji Temple** | Explore the large Sanmon gate and its expansive, famous Zen gardens (especially Hojo Garden). | 2.0 hrs | **Lunch:** Near Tofuku-ji Station. Look for a small restaurant serving *udon* or *soba*. |\\n| **14:00** | Travel to Central Kyoto/Nijo Area | Take a taxi or bus/subway combination. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **15:00** | **Nijo Castle** | Historical exploration of the Shogun\u2019s residence, famous \\\"nightingale floors.\\\" | 2.0 hrs | |\\n| **18:30** | Dinner near Kyoto Station or back in Gion | Explore *Kyoto Station Cube* for diverse dining options, or find traditional *Izakaya* fare. | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a54,000\u2013\u00a57,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 2 Budget Estimate:** Transport: \u00a51,500. Temple Entries: \u00a51,000 (Tofuku-ji + Nijo Castle). Lunch/Dinner: \u00a56,500. **Total: ~\u00a59,000**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 3: Bamboo Forests and Western Majesty (Arashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **08:00** | Travel to Arashiyama (via JR Sagano Line) | Early start is crucial for quiet contemplation. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **08:30** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | Walk through the towering bamboo forest. | 1.0 hr | |\\n| **09:30** | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** (UNESCO Site) | Explore the main temple and its spectacular landscape garden. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:30** | **Okochi Sanso Villa** (Optional but recommended) | Former actor\u2019s villa with stunning views and immaculate gardens (includes tea). | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **13:30** | Lunch in Arashiyama Village | Look for restaurants specializing in *Yudofu* (simmered tofu), an Arashiyama specialty. | 1.5 hrs | **Lunch:** \u00a52,500\u2013\u00a54,000 pp |\\n| **15:30** | Togetsukyo Bridge & Tenryu-ji Temple Garden stroll. | Gentle nature walk. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **18:30** | Return to Central Kyoto & Dinner | Relaxed dinner near the hotel. Try Okonomiyaki (savory pancake). | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a53,000\u2013\u00a55,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 3 Budget Estimate:** Transport (Round Trip): \u00a51,000. Entry Fees: \u00a51,600 (Tenryu-ji + Villa). Meals: \u00a57,000. **Total: ~\u00a59,600**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 4: Golden Splendor and Zen Contemplation (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:00** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | Arrive shortly after opening for the best light on the gold leaf. (Bus recommended). | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **10:30** | Travel to Ryoan-ji | Short bus ride or 20-min walk. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:00** | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | Contemplate the famous, enigmatic rock garden. | 1.0 hr | |\\n| **12:30** | Lunch near Ryoan-ji/Kitano | Look for local, simple lunch spots frequented by locals. | 1.0 hr | **Lunch:** \u00a51,500\u2013\u00a52,500 pp |\\n| **14:00** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | Explore several sub-temples and quiet moss gardens; a deep dive into Zen architecture. | 2.5 hrs | |\\n| **17:00** | **Tea Experience near Daitoku-ji:** Look for a smaller, local tea house outside the main complex. | Deeper immersion into tea culture. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner: Nishiki Market Area | Explore the area surrounding Kyoto's kitchen for fresh seafood or specialty dishes. | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a55,000\u2013\u00a58,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 4 Budget Estimate:** Transport (Bus Day Pass recommended): \u00a5700. Temple Entries: \u00a51,100. Tea Experience: \u00a53,500. Meals: \u00a57,000. **Total: ~\u00a512,300**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 5: Reflection and Philosophy (Higashiyama North)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:30** | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | Explore the moss gardens and dry sand gardens surrounding the pavilion. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:00** | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)** | Leisurely walk along the canal, stopping at small shrines along the way. | 2.0 hrs | **Snack:** Stop at a small cafe along the path for coffee or matcha ice cream. |\\n| **13:30** | Lunch in the Ginkaku-ji/Path area | Look for quieter, more refined lunch options. | 1.5 hrs | **Lunch:** \u00a52,000\u2013\u00a53,500 pp |\\n| **15:30** | **Formal Tea Ceremony (*Chaji* Intensive)** | Book a longer, comprehensive session (2-3 hours) to learn the full ritual and etiquette. | 3.0 hrs | Pre-book this well in advance. |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner & Gion Exploration | Casual dinner, then an evening stroll through Gion hoping for a glimpse of a *Geiko* or *Maiko*. (Remember etiquette!) | 2.5 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a54,000\u2013\u00a56,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 5 Budget Estimate:** Transport: \u00a51,000. Entry Fees: \u00a5500. Intensive Tea Ceremony: \u00a57,000\u2013\u00a510,000. Meals: \u00a56,500. **Total: ~\u00a515,000\u2013\u00a518,000** (Higher due to intensive tea class)\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 6: Hidden Gems and Artisanal Crafts\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:30** | **Heian Jingu Shrine** | Visit the massive *torii* gate and explore the impressive, colorful gardens behind the main hall. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:30** | **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design** (or Kyoto Museum of Traditional Crafts) | See demonstrations of weaving, dyeing, and pottery that maintain Kyoto\u2019s heritage. | 2.0 hrs | |\\n| **14:00** | Lunch in Downtown Kyoto (Kawaramachi/Shijo area) | Experience a modern aspect of Kyoto cuisine. | 1.5 hrs | **Lunch:** Try Kyoto-style ramen or *Gyukatsu*. |\\n| **16:00** | **Nishiki Market (Return Visit)** | Focus on tasting different local pickles (*tsukemono*), sweets, and street food for late afternoon nibbles. | 2.0 hrs | **Snack Focused** |\\n| **19:30** | Farewell Dinner: *Kaiseki* Experience | Splurge on a traditional multi-course *Kaiseki* meal (pre-booking essential). | 2.5 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a510,000+ pp |\\n\\n**Day 6 Budget Estimate:** Transport: \u00a51,000. Entry Fees: \u00a5600. Meals/Tasting: \u00a513,000 (including Kaiseki). **Total: ~\u00a514,600**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 7: Mountain Spirituality and Departure (Optional Hike/Departure)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Option A (Nature Hike)** | **Kurama-dera Temple & Kifune Shrine** | Take the Eizan Electric Railway north. Hike between the two mountain temples for a spiritual forest experience. (Requires 5-6 hours round trip plus travel). | Full Day | Lunch near Kifune Shrine (known for *Nagashi Somen* if in season). |\\n| **Option B (Relaxed Departure)** | Revisit a favorite spot (e.g., Ryoan-ji for quiet reflection) or visit **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** (1001 statues of Kannon). | Final sightseeing/Souvenir shopping. | 3.0 hrs | **Lunch:** Light lunch near Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Head to Kyoto Station, board transport for KIX/ITM or next destination. | Departure Logistics | 2.5 hrs | |\\n\\n**Day 7 Budget Estimate (Option B):** Transport: \u00a51,500. Entry: \u00a5800. Lunch: \u00a52,500. **Total: ~\u00a54,800 + Airport transfer**\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Budget Summary (Estimated Major Expenses)\\n\\nThis estimation excludes international flights but covers on-the-ground costs for one person based on the itinerary above, assuming mid-range dining choices (with one splurge meal).\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Average | Total 7-Day Estimate | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** | \u00a515,000 | \u00a590,000 (~$600 USD) | Based on mid-range hotel or high-quality Airbnb/Ryokan. |\\n| **Local Transport** | \u00a51,000 | \u00a57,000 | Covers buses, subways, and a few short taxi rides/train segments. |\\n| **Entry Fees/Activities** | \u00a51,500 | \u00a510,500 | Includes temple fees and one major specialized activity (Kaiseki excluded). |\\n| **Meals & Drinks** | \u00a59,000 | \u00a563,000 | Includes 6 dinners, 6 lunches, and daily snacks/drinks. |\\n| **Special Activities** | N/A | \u00a515,000 | Covers the intensive Tea Ceremony/Masterclass cost difference. |\\n| **Splurge Dinner (Kaiseki)** | N/A | \u00a512,000 | Allocated specifically for Day 6 splurge. |\\n| **Buffer/Souvenirs** | N/A | \u00a520,000 | Recommended for unforeseen purchases or emergencies. |\\n| **ESTIMATED TOTAL** | **~\u00a527,000/day** | **\u00a5217,500 (~$1,450 USD)** | *Excludes flights to/from Japan.* |\\n\\n    OPTIMIZATION TIPS: [your money-saving and practical tips here]\\n\\n    TRAVEL ESSENTIALS: [packing and preparation advice here]\\n\\n    BACKUP PLANS: [alternative options and contingencies here]\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"TravelOptimizerAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that optimizes travel plans with practical advice and alternatives\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]}}}},\"expected_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-8e149827-dcc4-4e6c-8b8c-6409e9319355\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"ITINERARY: This is Agent **ItineraryBuilderAgent**. I will now structure the detailed 7-day cultural deep-dive itinerary for Kyoto based on the provided research framework, enhanced with practical optimization tips.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto Itinerary (Optimized)\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on minimizing travel time by grouping activities geographically, maximizing exposure to temples, deep traditions (tea/Zen), and natural beauty, while integrating essential budget, packing, and contingency planning.\\n\\n## I. Trip Overview & Logistics (Unchanged)\\n\\n*(Logistics, Budget Estimates, and Suggested Accommodation Areas remain as detailed in the original plan.)*\\n\\n---\\n\\n## II. Daily Itinerary (Optimized with Budget & Contingency Notes)\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & Southern Icon \u2013 The Thousand Gates\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrival at KIX/ITM, transfer to Kyoto Station, check in. | Logistics | 3 hours | Grab quick *onigiri* or station bento box (*ekiben*). | **BUDGET TIP:** If flying into KIX, the **JR Haruka Express** is efficient but expensive. For savings, consider the *Limousine Bus* or the cheaper *JR Kansai Airport Rapid Service* to Osaka, then transfer to a local train to Kyoto. |\\n| **15:00** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** Exploration. | Temple/Nature | 3.5 hours | Hike at least halfway up. | **PACKING TIP:** Bring sturdy, comfortable walking shoes\u2014the path is uneven stone/dirt. |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner near accommodation. | Local Cuisine | 1.5 hours | **Dinner:** Local *Yakiniku* or ramen. | **LOCAL APP:** Download **Google Maps** (for real-time bus tracking) and **Japan Transit Planner** (Jorudan) for all intercity routes. |\\n\\n### Day 2: Eastern Majesty \u2013 History, View, and Tea\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Higashiyama District (East)*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **08:00** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** visit. | Iconic Temple | 1.5 hours | Must arrive early. | **BUDGET TIP:** Buy a Kyoto One-Day Bus Pass (\u00a5700) if you take 3+ bus rides today. |\\n| **09:30** | Walk down **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka**. | Historic Streets | 1.5 hours | Sample *yatsuhashi*. | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not block the pathway for photos on these narrow streets; tourists clogging traffic leads to resentment. |\\n| **14:00** | **Traditional Tea Ceremony Experience.** | Deep Tradition | 1.5 hours | Book in advance. | **CULTURAL DO:** Arrive silently. Observe how the host handles the utensils with reverence. |\\n| **19:00** | Dinner in Gion/Pontocho Alley. | Dining | 2 hours | Pontocho Alley dining. | **BACKUP PLAN (Gion):** If Pontocho is too crowded/expensive, pivot to Kawaramachi for a wider range of *Izakaya* options. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Northern Reflection \u2013 Zen and Gold\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Northern Kyoto*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **10:00** | **Kinkaku-ji** (Golden Pavilion). | Iconic Temple | 1.5 hours | Aim for morning sunlight. | **BUDGET TIP:** Kinkaku-ji is bus-dependent; use the One-Day Pass if traveling from Central Kyoto. |\\n| **11:30** | **Ry\u014dan-ji Temple.** | Zen Garden | 1 hour | Ponder the rock garden. | **CULTURAL DO:** Sit silently at the viewing veranda at Ry\u014dan-ji. It\u2019s not about *finding* meaning, but about *being* present. |\\n| **14:00** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex.** | Deeper Zen | 2 hours | Explore sub-temples. | **PACKING TIP:** Bring a small notepad/sketchbook to capture impressions of the less busy gardens here. |\\n| **17:30** | Walk a segment of the **Philosopher's Path**. | Nature/Reflection | 1 hour | Enjoy late afternoon light. | **BACKUP PLAN (Weather):** If it's raining, substitute the path walk with exploring the covered shopping arcades (Teramachi/Shinkyogoku) near Karasuma Oike. |\\n\\n### Day 4: Western Serenity \u2013 Bamboo & Vegetarian Cuisine\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Arashiyama (West)*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **07:00** | Travel to Arashiyama. | Transit | 45 min | | **LOCAL APP:** Use **Navitime** for highly detailed train/bus routing in Japan, especially when transferring between JR and private lines (like Hankyu for Arashiyama). |\\n| **08:00** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** | Nature Priority | 1 hour | **Crucial to arrive early.** | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not try to climb into the small adjacent garden areas near the main path for photos\u2014it\u2019s private property. |\\n| **12:30** | **Shojin Ryori Lunch.** | Culinary Tradition | 1.5 hours | Book *shojin ryori* at Shigetsu. | **BUDGET ALTERNATIVE:** If *shojin ryori* is too costly (it can be pricey), find a small shop serving **Tofu/Yudofu** (boiled tofu), a local Arashiyama specialty, which is lighter and cheaper. |\\n| **14:30** | Scenic **Randen Tram** back east. | Experience | 2 hours | | **TRANSPORT TIP:** The Randen Tram is a beautiful, slow ride\u2014a cheaper, more scenic alternative to the Sagano train. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Shogunate Power & Culinary Heart\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Central Kyoto*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:00** | **Nij\u014d Castle.** | History/Power | 2 hours | Nightingale Floors. | **PACKING TIP:** Bring a small coin purse (\u00a5100 and \u00a5500 coins are essential for many shrine/temple offerings and locker rentals). |\\n| **12:00** | Explore **Nishiki Market**. | Food Culture | 1.5 hours | Graze through samples. | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not eat or drink while walking through the market. Find a small bench or designated area to consume your purchase. |\\n| **15:30** | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple.** | Art/Worship | 1.5 hours | 1001 statues. | **BACKUP PLAN (Interior Focus):** As this is an indoor activity, it\u2019s perfect for a rainy afternoon. Photography is strictly forbidden inside. |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner in Central Kyoto. | Kyoto Specialty | 2 hours | Try *Kaiseki* or *Saba Zushi*. | **BUDGET TIP (Kaiseki):** If you want the *Kaiseki* experience without the full dinner price, look for *Kaiseki Lunch Sets* on Day 6 instead. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Deep Dive or Day Trip Exploration\\n\\n*Flex Day: Deep Dive vs. Uji Day Trip*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Option A (Kyoto):** Nanzen-ji/T\u014d-ji | Zen/Pagoda | Full Day | Lunch near Nanzen-ji. | **BUDGET TIP:** Visit **T\u014d-ji Temple** on the first Sunday of the month if your trip aligns, as it hosts a massive flea market (*Kobo-san* Market) which is free to enter and great for cultural browsing. |\\n| **Option B (Uji Day Trip):** | Tea Focus | Full Day | Matcha Soba/Sweets in Uji. | **TRANSPORT TIP:** Uji is easily accessible on the JR Nara Line (covered by a JR Pass if you have one). If you don't have a pass, ensure you use your IC card. |\\n| **19:30** | Farewell Dinner back in Kyoto. | Celebration | 2 hours | Splurge or revisit. | **CULTURAL DO:** Consider purchasing a small, locally crafted item (pottery, incense) near your favorite temple as a respectful memento. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Reflection and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:00** | Final quiet morning walk. | Reflection | 1.5 hours | | **PACKING TIP:** If you bought liquids/perishables, use the luggage forwarding service (**Takuhaibin**) to send them ahead to the airport or your next hotel for hassle-free departure. |\\n| **10:30** | Last Matcha fix and souvenir gathering. | Last Stops | 1.5 hours | Final coffee/breakfast. | **BUDGET/TIME SAVER:** Buy standardized souvenirs (matcha powder, green tea sweets) at Kyoto Station, which is faster than browsing small shops. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## IV. Essential Recommendations for Your Cultural Deep Dive\\n\\n### 1. Money-Saving Tips & Budget Alternatives\\n\\n*   **IC Card Strategy:** Purchase an **ICOCA card** immediately upon arrival. While it doesn't offer discounts, it saves significant time compared to buying individual paper tickets for every bus/train transfer.\\n*   **Temple Hopping:** Instead of paying entry fees daily, look for temples that offer special compound passes or explore the *many* free shrines (like Yasaka Shrine).\\n*   **Lunch vs. Dinner:** Japanese fine dining (like *Kaiseki*) is dramatically cheaper at lunchtime. If you want to experience high cuisine, opt for a **Kaiseki Lunch Set** (often 40-50% cheaper than the dinner equivalent).\\n*   **Convenience Store Gems:** For extremely cheap, high-quality breakfast/snacks, utilize 7-Eleven, Lawson, or Family Mart for coffee, sandwiches, and *onigiri*.\\n\\n### 2. Packing Recommendations Specific to Kyoto\\n\\n| Item | Purpose | Cultural/Practical Context |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Comfortable Shoes** | Essential for walking 5\u201310 miles/day on uneven temple paths. | You will be removing shoes frequently; slip-on styles are highly recommended over complicated boots/laces. |\\n| **Layers (Breathable)** | Weather can shift quickly, especially in Spring/Autumn. | Temples are often cool indoors, even if sunny outside. |\\n| **Small Towel/Handkerchief** | Essential for drying hands after using public washrooms or purification fountains. | Public restrooms rarely have paper towels or air dryers. |\\n| **Portable Power Bank** | For constant use of navigation apps (Google Maps, Jorudan). | Kyoto's bus routes are complex; phone battery life is critical. |\\n| **Light Jacket/Rain Shell** | For unpredictable shoulder season weather. | A light, foldable umbrella is easily purchased locally, but a quality waterproof shell is better protection. |\\n\\n### 3. Backup Plans for Weather or Unexpected Situations\\n\\n| Situation | Primary Backup Plan | Cultural/Traditional Alternative |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Heavy Rain/Typhoon** | Focus on Central Kyoto (Nij\u014d Castle, Nishiki Market covered arcades, museums like the Kyoto National Museum). | Attend a traditional craft workshop (pottery, dyeing) held indoors. |\\n| **Extreme Heat/Humidity** | Switch to air-conditioned spaces: Kyoto Station complex (shopping/restaurants), large department stores, or underground shopping malls. | Schedule temple visits for first thing in the morning (7:30 AM) and rest during the 1 PM - 4 PM heat. |\\n| **Major Holiday Crowds (e.g., Golden Week)** | Visit Kyoto\u2019s less-trafficked Zen temples (e.g., Daitokuji sub-temples) or take the Uji day trip (Day 6) early in the morning. | Book all transportation and dinner reservations *at least* 4-6 weeks in advance. |\\n\\n### 4. Local Apps, Websites, or Resources to Download\\n\\n1.  **Google Maps:** Indispensable for real-time bus locations and routes.\\n2.  **Japan Transit Planner (Jorudan) / Navitime:** Best for checking train schedules, platform numbers, and complex transfers.\\n3.  **Google Translate:** Essential, especially for reading small menu items or complex signs at smaller, family-run shops.\\n4.  **Kyoto Bus Navi (Official):** Very helpful for understanding specific bus routes, which cover most tourist sites.\\n\\n### 5. Cultural Do's and Don'ts for Respectful Travel\\n\\n| DO | DON'T |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **DO** Carry a small bag for items removed at temples (e.g., shoes, small purchases). | **DON\u2019T** Take photos of private residences or *Maiko/Geiko* without explicit, clear permission. |\\n| **DO** Say *Sumimasen* (excuse me) before squeezing past someone on a busy train or narrow path. | **DON\u2019T** Eat or drink while walking through the streets or on public transit (unless on the Shinkansen). |\\n| **DO** Use both hands when giving or receiving items (especially money, business cards, or gifts). | **DON\u2019T** Point at people, artifacts, or religious statues with your finger. Use an open hand gesture. |\\n| **DO** Bow slightly when thanking staff or receiving change. | **DON\u2019T** Leave tips\u2014it can be interpreted as confusing or even insulting. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"tool_uses\":[],\"tool_responses\":[],\"intermediate_responses\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640021.829202,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":null},\"eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":1.0,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":1.0,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.5664880662445202,\"eval_status\":2,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}]}],\"session_id\":\"___eval___session___4662802f-ab71-4131-a84e-e615736826f2\",\"session_details\":{\"id\":\"___eval___session___4662802f-ab71-4131-a84e-e615736826f2\",\"app_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent\",\"user_id\":\"user\",\"state\":{\"travel_itinerary\":\"## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive: Kyoto Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on maximizing your cultural immersion, balancing iconic sightseeing with authentic traditional experiences, structured geographically to minimize travel time.\\n\\n**Agent Name:** ItineraryBuilderAgent\\n\\n### Trip Overview\\n\\n*   **Duration:** 7 Days / 6 Nights\\n*   **Focus:** Temples, Shrines, Zen Gardens, Traditional Arts (Tea Ceremony), and Nature.\\n*   **Best Travel Style:** Public Transport (Bus/Subway) supplemented by walking. An ICOCA card is highly recommended.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\n**Gion/Higashiyama District:** This area is ideal for a cultural deep-dive. It keeps you close to historic streets (Ninenzaka, Sannenzaka), traditional restaurants, and many major eastern temples. It allows for early morning or late evening strolls in atmospheric neighborhoods.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Daily Itinerary Breakdown\\n\\n#### Day 1: Arrival and Eastern Gateway (Higashiyama South)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrive at KIX/ITM, take Haruka Express/Limo bus to Kyoto Station. Check into accommodation. | Logistics & Settling In | 3.5 hrs | Quick lunch near your hotel or Kyoto Station. |\\n| **14:00** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** (UNESCO Site) | Iconic views, wooden stage. (Visit slightly later to avoid peak crowds) | 2.0 hrs | |\\n| **16:30** | Walk down **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** Streets | Historic preserved streets, traditional shops. | 1.5 hrs | Try *Yatsuhashi* (sweet confection) samples. |\\n| **18:00** | **Early Evening Tea Ceremony:** Book a short, introductory ceremony in the Higashiyama area. | Tradition/Etiquette | 1.0 hr | |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner in Pontocho Alley | Atmospheric narrow alley known for dining. Try *Kushikatsu* (deep-fried skewers). | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a53,500\u2013\u00a56,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 1 Budget Estimate:** Local Transport (Train/Bus to hotel): \u00a53,500. Temple Entry: \u00a5400. Tea Ceremony: \u00a53,000. Dinner: \u00a54,500. **Total: ~\u00a511,400**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 2: Thousand Gates and Zen Serenity (South Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **07:30** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** Hike | Iconic *Torii* gates. Go early to avoid severe crowds and heat/sun. | 3.0 hrs | **Breakfast:** Grab snacks (*Inari sushi* or rice balls) from a convenience store near the shrine entrance. |\\n| **11:00** | Travel to Tofuku-ji Area | Transition via JR Nara Line or Keihan Line. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:30** | **Tofuku-ji Temple** | Explore the large Sanmon gate and its expansive, famous Zen gardens (especially Hojo Garden). | 2.0 hrs | **Lunch:** Near Tofuku-ji Station. Look for a small restaurant serving *udon* or *soba*. |\\n| **14:00** | Travel to Central Kyoto/Nijo Area | Take a taxi or bus/subway combination. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **15:00** | **Nijo Castle** | Historical exploration of the Shogun\u2019s residence, famous \\\"nightingale floors.\\\" | 2.0 hrs | |\\n| **18:30** | Dinner near Kyoto Station or back in Gion | Explore *Kyoto Station Cube* for diverse dining options, or find traditional *Izakaya* fare. | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a54,000\u2013\u00a57,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 2 Budget Estimate:** Transport: \u00a51,500. Temple Entries: \u00a51,000 (Tofuku-ji + Nijo Castle). Lunch/Dinner: \u00a56,500. **Total: ~\u00a59,000**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 3: Bamboo Forests and Western Majesty (Arashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **08:00** | Travel to Arashiyama (via JR Sagano Line) | Early start is crucial for quiet contemplation. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **08:30** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | Walk through the towering bamboo forest. | 1.0 hr | |\\n| **09:30** | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** (UNESCO Site) | Explore the main temple and its spectacular landscape garden. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:30** | **Okochi Sanso Villa** (Optional but recommended) | Former actor\u2019s villa with stunning views and immaculate gardens (includes tea). | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **13:30** | Lunch in Arashiyama Village | Look for restaurants specializing in *Yudofu* (simmered tofu), an Arashiyama specialty. | 1.5 hrs | **Lunch:** \u00a52,500\u2013\u00a54,000 pp |\\n| **15:30** | Togetsukyo Bridge & Tenryu-ji Temple Garden stroll. | Gentle nature walk. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **18:30** | Return to Central Kyoto & Dinner | Relaxed dinner near the hotel. Try Okonomiyaki (savory pancake). | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a53,000\u2013\u00a55,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 3 Budget Estimate:** Transport (Round Trip): \u00a51,000. Entry Fees: \u00a51,600 (Tenryu-ji + Villa). Meals: \u00a57,000. **Total: ~\u00a59,600**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 4: Golden Splendor and Zen Contemplation (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:00** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | Arrive shortly after opening for the best light on the gold leaf. (Bus recommended). | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **10:30** | Travel to Ryoan-ji | Short bus ride or 20-min walk. | 0.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:00** | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | Contemplate the famous, enigmatic rock garden. | 1.0 hr | |\\n| **12:30** | Lunch near Ryoan-ji/Kitano | Look for local, simple lunch spots frequented by locals. | 1.0 hr | **Lunch:** \u00a51,500\u2013\u00a52,500 pp |\\n| **14:00** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | Explore several sub-temples and quiet moss gardens; a deep dive into Zen architecture. | 2.5 hrs | |\\n| **17:00** | **Tea Experience near Daitoku-ji:** Look for a smaller, local tea house outside the main complex. | Deeper immersion into tea culture. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner: Nishiki Market Area | Explore the area surrounding Kyoto's kitchen for fresh seafood or specialty dishes. | 2.0 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a55,000\u2013\u00a58,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 4 Budget Estimate:** Transport (Bus Day Pass recommended): \u00a5700. Temple Entries: \u00a51,100. Tea Experience: \u00a53,500. Meals: \u00a57,000. **Total: ~\u00a512,300**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 5: Reflection and Philosophy (Higashiyama North)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:30** | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | Explore the moss gardens and dry sand gardens surrounding the pavilion. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:00** | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)** | Leisurely walk along the canal, stopping at small shrines along the way. | 2.0 hrs | **Snack:** Stop at a small cafe along the path for coffee or matcha ice cream. |\\n| **13:30** | Lunch in the Ginkaku-ji/Path area | Look for quieter, more refined lunch options. | 1.5 hrs | **Lunch:** \u00a52,000\u2013\u00a53,500 pp |\\n| **15:30** | **Formal Tea Ceremony (*Chaji* Intensive)** | Book a longer, comprehensive session (2-3 hours) to learn the full ritual and etiquette. | 3.0 hrs | Pre-book this well in advance. |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner & Gion Exploration | Casual dinner, then an evening stroll through Gion hoping for a glimpse of a *Geiko* or *Maiko*. (Remember etiquette!) | 2.5 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a54,000\u2013\u00a56,000 pp |\\n\\n**Day 5 Budget Estimate:** Transport: \u00a51,000. Entry Fees: \u00a5500. Intensive Tea Ceremony: \u00a57,000\u2013\u00a510,000. Meals: \u00a56,500. **Total: ~\u00a515,000\u2013\u00a518,000** (Higher due to intensive tea class)\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 6: Hidden Gems and Artisanal Crafts\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:30** | **Heian Jingu Shrine** | Visit the massive *torii* gate and explore the impressive, colorful gardens behind the main hall. | 1.5 hrs | |\\n| **11:30** | **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design** (or Kyoto Museum of Traditional Crafts) | See demonstrations of weaving, dyeing, and pottery that maintain Kyoto\u2019s heritage. | 2.0 hrs | |\\n| **14:00** | Lunch in Downtown Kyoto (Kawaramachi/Shijo area) | Experience a modern aspect of Kyoto cuisine. | 1.5 hrs | **Lunch:** Try Kyoto-style ramen or *Gyukatsu*. |\\n| **16:00** | **Nishiki Market (Return Visit)** | Focus on tasting different local pickles (*tsukemono*), sweets, and street food for late afternoon nibbles. | 2.0 hrs | **Snack Focused** |\\n| **19:30** | Farewell Dinner: *Kaiseki* Experience | Splurge on a traditional multi-course *Kaiseki* meal (pre-booking essential). | 2.5 hrs | **Budget:** \u00a510,000+ pp |\\n\\n**Day 6 Budget Estimate:** Transport: \u00a51,000. Entry Fees: \u00a5600. Meals/Tasting: \u00a513,000 (including Kaiseki). **Total: ~\u00a514,600**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### Day 7: Mountain Spirituality and Departure (Optional Hike/Departure)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Option A (Nature Hike)** | **Kurama-dera Temple & Kifune Shrine** | Take the Eizan Electric Railway north. Hike between the two mountain temples for a spiritual forest experience. (Requires 5-6 hours round trip plus travel). | Full Day | Lunch near Kifune Shrine (known for *Nagashi Somen* if in season). |\\n| **Option B (Relaxed Departure)** | Revisit a favorite spot (e.g., Ryoan-ji for quiet reflection) or visit **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** (1001 statues of Kannon). | Final sightseeing/Souvenir shopping. | 3.0 hrs | **Lunch:** Light lunch near Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Head to Kyoto Station, board transport for KIX/ITM or next destination. | Departure Logistics | 2.5 hrs | |\\n\\n**Day 7 Budget Estimate (Option B):** Transport: \u00a51,500. Entry: \u00a5800. Lunch: \u00a52,500. **Total: ~\u00a54,800 + Airport transfer**\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Budget Summary (Estimated Major Expenses)\\n\\nThis estimation excludes international flights but covers on-the-ground costs for one person based on the itinerary above, assuming mid-range dining choices (with one splurge meal).\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Average | Total 7-Day Estimate | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** | \u00a515,000 | \u00a590,000 (~$600 USD) | Based on mid-range hotel or high-quality Airbnb/Ryokan. |\\n| **Local Transport** | \u00a51,000 | \u00a57,000 | Covers buses, subways, and a few short taxi rides/train segments. |\\n| **Entry Fees/Activities** | \u00a51,500 | \u00a510,500 | Includes temple fees and one major specialized activity (Kaiseki excluded). |\\n| **Meals & Drinks** | \u00a59,000 | \u00a563,000 | Includes 6 dinners, 6 lunches, and daily snacks/drinks. |\\n| **Special Activities** | N/A | \u00a515,000 | Covers the intensive Tea Ceremony/Masterclass cost difference. |\\n| **Splurge Dinner (Kaiseki)** | N/A | \u00a512,000 | Allocated specifically for Day 6 splurge. |\\n| **Buffer/Souvenirs** | N/A | \u00a520,000 | Recommended for unforeseen purchases or emergencies. |\\n| **ESTIMATED TOTAL** | **~\u00a527,000/day** | **\u00a5217,500 (~$1,450 USD)** | *Excludes flights to/from Japan.* |\",\"__llm_request_key__\":\"7e01377d-b3fc-440d-9617-5e6b9f18c8c1\",\"destination_research\":\"It sounds like a wonderful trip! Kyoto is the perfect city for a deep dive into Japanese culture.\\n\\nHere is a comprehensive guide based on your 7-day cultural immersion preference.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto: Temples, Traditions, Tea & Nature\\n\\n### 1. Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto are **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**, as these seasons offer the most pleasant weather for sightseeing and beautiful natural scenery.\\n\\n*   **Spring (March-May):** Famous for the **cherry blossoms** (late March to early April). The weather is generally warm and comfortable, though rain is possible, especially toward late May. *Be aware of the major crowds and higher costs during this peak season, particularly around Golden Week (late April/early May).*\\n*   **Autumn (October-November):** Excellent weather with sunny, warm days and cool evenings, perfect for exploring. This season is renowned for its spectacular **fall foliage**, peaking in November. November rivals May as one of the best months.\\n*   **Summer (June-August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season generally running from mid-June to late July.\\n*   **Winter (December-February):** Cold, but offers a serene atmosphere with fewer crowds and occasional snow.\\n\\n### 2. Top Attractions & Must-See Locations (Temple & Nature Focus)\\n\\nKyoto boasts over 2,000 religious structures, including 17 UNESCO World Heritage Sites. This itinerary prioritizes key cultural and natural spots:\\n\\n| Category | Must-See Locations | Cultural Significance / Nature Highlight |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Iconic Temples** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | UNESCO site; stunning gold-leaf covered Zen temple reflected in a pond. |\\n| | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | World Heritage Site with a historic wooden veranda overlooking the city. |\\n| | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | Iconic Shinto shrine with thousands of vibrant red *torii* gates climbing a mountain trail. |\\n| | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | Known for its superb Zen garden, though often crowded. |\\n| | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | UNESCO World Heritage Site in Arashiyama; features one of the city's oldest and most celebrated landscape gardens. |\\n| **Zen & Serenity** | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | Famous for its world-renowned, enigmatic rock garden. |\\n| | **Tofuku-ji Temple** | Home to the oldest *sanmon* gate in Japan and beautiful rock gardens. |\\n| | **Daitoku-ji** | A sprawling, secluded Zen temple complex known for peaceful sculpted landscapes. |\\n| **Nature & Scenery** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | A must-see natural area, best visited early morning. |\\n| | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)** | A scenic path, particularly lovely during cherry blossom season. |\\n\\n### 3. Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nRespecting local traditions will greatly enrich your experience, especially when visiting sacred sites.\\n\\n*   **At Temples and Shrines:**\\n    *   **Show Respect:** Bend slightly when entering temple grounds as a sign of respect.\\n    *   **Purification Fountain (*Temizuya*):** Cleanse your hands and mouth before entering. Use the ladle (*hishaku*) first for your left hand, then your right, then pour water into your left palm to rinse your mouth (do not let the ladle touch your lips). Finally, rinse the ladle handle.\\n    *   **Footwear:** Be prepared to remove your outdoor shoes before entering temple buildings, especially those with *tatami* flooring.\\n    *   **Photography:** Observe signs carefully; photography may be prohibited inside buildings or in certain areas. Avoid using flash.\\n    *   **Noise & Conduct:** Keep voices down; temples are places for prayer and worship. Do not eat or drink inside temples/shrines.\\n*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is not customary in Japan.\\n    *   **Littering:** Do not litter; use designated trash areas.\\n    *   **Queuing:** Always queue in an orderly line when waiting for transport or entering popular spots.\\n    *   **Walking:** Be mindful not to block the way of pedestrians when walking in groups on narrow streets.\\n    *   **Smoking:** Only smoke in designated areas; never smoke while walking.\\n\\n### 4. Transportation Options\\n\\nKyoto has an excellent, yet sometimes crowded, public transport network.\\n\\n*   **Buses:** Considered the **best way to get around** for reaching many central attractions not near train stations (e.g., Kinkaku-ji, Ryoan-ji).\\n    *   *Tip:* Enter at the back door and exit/pay at the front door.\\n*   **Subway & Trains:** Two subway lines (Karasuma N-S, Tozai E-W) provide fast access across the city. Railways connect to major hubs like Arashiyama and Fushimi Inari.\\n*   **IC Cards:** Using an IC card (like ICOCA, Suica, or PASMO) is the easiest way to pay for virtually all trains and buses.\\n*   **Passes:** Consider a **Subway & Bus One-Day Pass** for unlimited travel on both systems if you plan extensive travel in one day.\\n*   **Walking & Biking:** Kyoto is relatively compact and flat, making walking and cycling great for exploring historic districts (like Gion/Higashiyama) or scenic paths.\\n\\n### 5. Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is generally considered one of the safest cities in the world, with very low crime rates.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** You can safely walk the streets at night, even as a solo traveler. Exercise common sense, such as keeping an eye on your belongings in crowded markets or on public transport.\\n*   **Emergency Numbers:**\\n    *   Police: **110**\\n    *   Medical Emergency/Ambulance: **119**\\n*   **Natural Risks:** Be aware that the risk of natural disasters, such as earthquakes, is considered high. If venturing into mountain areas, check for recent **Asiatic black bear** sightings and take precautions like traveling in groups or carrying noise-making items.\\n*   **Travel Requirements:** Check current visa and vaccination requirements for Japan prior to booking. Routine vaccinations are always wise.\\n*   **Geiko/Maiko Etiquette:** If you spot a *Geiko* (Geisha) or *Maiko* in districts like Gion, be respectful; do not swarm them or take photos without explicit permission, as they are often on their way to appointments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Suggested 7-Day Cultural Focus Itinerary Snippet\\n\\nFor your 7-day cultural deep-dive, focus your days geographically:\\n\\n*   **Day 1 (Arrival & Eastern Kyoto I):** Explore **Kiyomizu-dera** early, walk down the historic **Ninenzaka/Sannenzaka** streets, and perhaps participate in an early evening **tea ceremony** in the Higashiyama area (near Camellia Tea House).\\n*   **Day 2 (Southern Kyoto):** Visit **Fushimi Inari Taisha** for a morning hike through the *torii* gates, then explore the Zen garden and potentially a simple tea service at **Tofuku-ji Temple**.\\n*   **Day 3 (Western Kyoto - Arashiyama):** Take the train to Arashiyama. Visit **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** and its garden, followed by a contemplative walk through the **Bamboo Grove**.\\n*   **Day 4 (Northern Kyoto):** Visit **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** first thing in the morning. Afterwards, visit **Ryoan-ji Temple** for quiet contemplation of its rock garden. Consider a casual tea experience near Kinkaku-ji at a venue like Tea Ceremony KOTO.\\n*   **Day 5 (Tradition & Tea Masterclass):** Dedicate time to a more formal, longer **tea ceremony (*Chaji*)** experience or a private session to fully understand the tradition. Explore **Ginkaku-ji** and walk part of the **Philosopher's Path**.\\n*   **Day 6 (Central/Zen Deep Dive):** Visit **Nijo Castle** for history, then explore the quieter **Daitoku-ji** complex for Zen gardens and perhaps a meditation/tea experience.\\n*   **Day 7 (Nature & Reflection):** A longer nature day. If time/interest allows, take the train/bus out to **Kurama-dera** (a mountain temple) for hiking and spiritual connection, which can be combined with a visit to Kifune Shrine area.\"},\"events\":[{\"model_version\":null,\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"grounding_metadata\":null,\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":null,\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":null,\"usage_metadata\":null,\"live_session_resumption_update\":null,\"input_transcription\":null,\"output_transcription\":null,\"avg_logprobs\":null,\"logprobs_result\":null,\"cache_metadata\":null,\"citation_metadata\":null,\"invocation_id\":\"e-bc424be7-25db-49d9-a3d3-9e4d18eb07bc\",\"author\":\"user\",\"actions\":{\"skip_summarization\":null,\"state_delta\":{},\"artifact_delta\":{},\"transfer_to_agent\":null,\"escalate\":null,\"requested_auth_configs\":{},\"requested_tool_confirmations\":{},\"compaction\":null,\"end_of_agent\":null,\"agent_state\":null,\"rewind_before_invocation_id\":null},\"long_running_tool_ids\":null,\"branch\":null,\"id\":\"50a89989-b533-46a1-82dc-3358cd674621\",\"timestamp\":1763640809.278668},{\"model_version\":\"gemini-2.5-flash-lite-preview-09-2025\",\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"It sounds like a wonderful trip! Kyoto is the perfect city for a deep dive into Japanese culture.\\n\\nHere is a comprehensive guide based on your 7-day cultural immersion preference.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto: Temples, Traditions, Tea & Nature\\n\\n### 1. Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto are **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**, as these seasons offer the most pleasant weather for sightseeing and beautiful natural scenery.\\n\\n*   **Spring (March-May):** Famous for the **cherry blossoms** (late March to early April). The weather is generally warm and comfortable, though rain is possible, especially toward late May. *Be aware of the major crowds and higher costs during this peak season, particularly around Golden Week (late April/early May).*\\n*   **Autumn (October-November):** Excellent weather with sunny, warm days and cool evenings, perfect for exploring. This season is renowned for its spectacular **fall foliage**, peaking in November. November rivals May as one of the best months.\\n*   **Summer (June-August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season generally running from mid-June to late July.\\n*   **Winter (December-February):** Cold, but offers a serene atmosphere with fewer crowds and occasional snow.\\n\\n### 2. Top Attractions & Must-See Locations (Temple & Nature Focus)\\n\\nKyoto boasts over 2,000 religious structures, including 17 UNESCO World Heritage Sites. This itinerary prioritizes key cultural and natural spots:\\n\\n| Category | Must-See Locations | Cultural Significance / Nature Highlight |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Iconic Temples** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | UNESCO site; stunning gold-leaf covered Zen temple reflected in a pond. |\\n| | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | World Heritage Site with a historic wooden veranda overlooking the city. |\\n| | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | Iconic Shinto shrine with thousands of vibrant red *torii* gates climbing a mountain trail. |\\n| | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | Known for its superb Zen garden, though often crowded. |\\n| | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | UNESCO World Heritage Site in Arashiyama; features one of the city's oldest and most celebrated landscape gardens. |\\n| **Zen & Serenity** | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | Famous for its world-renowned, enigmatic rock garden. |\\n| | **Tofuku-ji Temple** | Home to the oldest *sanmon* gate in Japan and beautiful rock gardens. |\\n| | **Daitoku-ji** | A sprawling, secluded Zen temple complex known for peaceful sculpted landscapes. |\\n| **Nature & Scenery** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | A must-see natural area, best visited early morning. |\\n| | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)** | A scenic path, particularly lovely during cherry blossom season. |\\n\\n### 3. Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nRespecting local traditions will greatly enrich your experience, especially when visiting sacred sites.\\n\\n*   **At Temples and Shrines:**\\n    *   **Show Respect:** Bend slightly when entering temple grounds as a sign of respect.\\n    *   **Purification Fountain (*Temizuya*):** Cleanse your hands and mouth before entering. Use the ladle (*hishaku*) first for your left hand, then your right, then pour water into your left palm to rinse your mouth (do not let the ladle touch your lips). Finally, rinse the ladle handle.\\n    *   **Footwear:** Be prepared to remove your outdoor shoes before entering temple buildings, especially those with *tatami* flooring.\\n    *   **Photography:** Observe signs carefully; photography may be prohibited inside buildings or in certain areas. Avoid using flash.\\n    *   **Noise & Conduct:** Keep voices down; temples are places for prayer and worship. Do not eat or drink inside temples/shrines.\\n*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is not customary in Japan.\\n    *   **Littering:** Do not litter; use designated trash areas.\\n    *   **Queuing:** Always queue in an orderly line when waiting for transport or entering popular spots.\\n    *   **Walking:** Be mindful not to block the way of pedestrians when walking in groups on narrow streets.\\n    *   **Smoking:** Only smoke in designated areas; never smoke while walking.\\n\\n### 4. Transportation Options\\n\\nKyoto has an excellent, yet sometimes crowded, public transport network.\\n\\n*   **Buses:** Considered the **best way to get around** for reaching many central attractions not near train stations (e.g., Kinkaku-ji, Ryoan-ji).\\n    *   *Tip:* Enter at the back door and exit/pay at the front door.\\n*   **Subway & Trains:** Two subway lines (Karasuma N-S, Tozai E-W) provide fast access across the city. Railways connect to major hubs like Arashiyama and Fushimi Inari.\\n*   **IC Cards:** Using an IC card (like ICOCA, Suica, or PASMO) is the easiest way to pay for virtually all trains and buses.\\n*   **Passes:** Consider a **Subway & Bus One-Day Pass** for unlimited travel on both systems if you plan extensive travel in one day.\\n*   **Walking & Biking:** Kyoto is relatively compact and flat, making walking and cycling great for exploring historic districts (like Gion/Higashiyama) or scenic paths.\\n\\n### 5. Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is generally considered one of the safest cities in the world, with very low crime rates.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** You can safely walk the streets at night, even as a solo traveler. Exercise common sense, such as keeping an eye on your belongings in crowded markets or on public transport.\\n*   **Emergency Numbers:**\\n    *   Police: **110**\\n    *   Medical Emergency/Ambulance: **119**\\n*   **Natural Risks:** Be aware that the risk of natural disasters, such as earthquakes, is considered high. If venturing into mountain areas, check for recent **Asiatic black bear** sightings and take precautions like traveling in groups or carrying noise-making items.\\n*   **Travel Requirements:** Check current visa and vaccination requirements for Japan prior to booking. Routine vaccinations are always wise.\\n*   **Geiko/Maiko Etiquette:** If you spot a *Geiko* (Geisha) or *Maiko* in districts like Gion, be respectful; do not swarm them or take photos without explicit permission, as they are often on their way to appointments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Suggested 7-Day Cultural Focus Itinerary Snippet\\n\\nFor your 7-day cultural deep-dive, focus your days geographically:\\n\\n*   **Day 1 (Arrival & Eastern Kyoto I):** Explore **Kiyomizu-dera** early, walk down the historic **Ninenzaka/Sannenzaka** streets, and perhaps participate in an early evening **tea ceremony** in the Higashiyama area (near Camellia Tea House).\\n*   **Day 2 (Southern Kyoto):** Visit **Fushimi Inari Taisha** for a morning hike through the *torii* gates, then explore the Zen garden and potentially a simple tea service at **Tofuku-ji Temple**.\\n*   **Day 3 (Western Kyoto - Arashiyama):** Take the train to Arashiyama. Visit **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** and its garden, followed by a contemplative walk through the **Bamboo Grove**.\\n*   **Day 4 (Northern Kyoto):** Visit **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** first thing in the morning. Afterwards, visit **Ryoan-ji Temple** for quiet contemplation of its rock garden. Consider a casual tea experience near Kinkaku-ji at a venue like Tea Ceremony KOTO.\\n*   **Day 5 (Tradition & Tea Masterclass):** Dedicate time to a more formal, longer **tea ceremony (*Chaji*)** experience or a private session to fully understand the tradition. Explore **Ginkaku-ji** and walk part of the **Philosopher's Path**.\\n*   **Day 6 (Central/Zen Deep Dive):** Visit **Nijo Castle** for history, then explore the quieter **Daitoku-ji** complex for Zen gardens and perhaps a meditation/tea experience.\\n*   **Day 7 (Nature & Reflection):** A longer nature day. If time/interest allows, take the train/bus out to **Kurama-dera** (a mountain temple) for hiking and spiritual connection, which can be combined with a visit to Kifune Shrine area.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"grounding_metadata\":{\"goo