unk_indices\":[0,1,2,3],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":500,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":307,\"text\":\"The best times to visit Kyoto are **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**, as these seasons offer the most pleasant weather for sightseeing and beautiful natural scenery.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[1,3],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":693,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":594,\"text\":\"The weather is generally warm and comfortable, though rain is possible, especially toward late May.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[1,2],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":822,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":821,\"text\":\"*\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[0,1,4],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1022,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":940,\"text\":\"This season is renowned for its spectacular **fall foliage**, peaking in November.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[0],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1069,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1023,\"text\":\"November rivals May as one of the best months.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[0,1],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1180,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1070,\"text\":\"*   **Summer (June-August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season generally running from mid-June to late July.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[4],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1292,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1181,\"text\":\"*   **Winter (December-February):** Cold, but offers a serene atmosphere with fewer crowds and occasional snow.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[3],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1450,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1363,\"text\":\"Kyoto boasts over 2,000 religious structures, including 17 UNESCO World Heritage Sites.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5,6,7],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1740,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1511,\"text\":\"| Category | Must-See Locations | Cultural Significance / Nature Highlight |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Iconic Temples** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | UNESCO site; stunning gold-leaf covered Zen temple reflected in a pond.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5,6,8,9],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1846,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1741,\"text\":\"|\\n| | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | World Heritage Site with a historic wooden veranda overlooking the city.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5,7],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":1971,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1847,\"text\":\"|\\n| | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** | Iconic Shinto shrine with thousands of vibrant red *torii* gates climbing a mountain trail.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[9],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2067,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":1972,\"text\":\"|\\n| | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | Known for its superb Zen garden, though often crowded.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[6,7],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2212,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2068,\"text\":\"|\\n| | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | UNESCO World Heritage Site in Arashiyama; features one of the city's oldest and most celebrated landscape gardens.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[8,9],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2313,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2213,\"text\":\"|\\n| **Zen & Serenity** | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | Famous for its world-renowned, enigmatic rock garden.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2412,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2314,\"text\":\"|\\n| | **Tofuku-ji Temple** | Home to the oldest *sanmon* gate in Japan and beautiful rock gardens.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[6,10],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2516,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2413,\"text\":\"|\\n| | **Daitoku-ji** | A sprawling, secluded Zen temple complex known for peaceful sculpted landscapes.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[11],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2626,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2517,\"text\":\"|\\n| **Nature & Scenery** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | A must-see natural area, best visited early morning.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[7],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2743,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2627,\"text\":\"|\\n| | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku no Michi)** | A scenic path, particularly lovely during cherry blossom season.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[12],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":2901,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2798,\"text\":\"Respecting local traditions will greatly enrich your experience, especially when visiting sacred sites.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[12],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3025,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":2903,\"text\":\"*   **At Temples and Shrines:**\\n    *   **Show Respect:** Bend slightly when entering temple grounds as a sign of respect.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[12],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3317,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3285,\"text\":\"Finally, rinse the ladle handle.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[13,12],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3455,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3322,\"text\":\"*   **Footwear:** Be prepared to remove your outdoor shoes before entering temple buildings, especially those with *tatami* flooring.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[12,14],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3592,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3574,\"text\":\"Avoid using flash.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[12,14],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3726,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3683,\"text\":\"Do not eat or drink inside temples/shrines.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[13],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3809,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3727,\"text\":\"*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is not customary in Japan.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[13,14],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3875,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3814,\"text\":\"*   **Littering:** Do not litter; use designated trash areas.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[13,15,16],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":3982,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3880,\"text\":\"*   **Queuing:** Always queue in an orderly line when waiting for transport or entering popular spots.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[14],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4092,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":3987,\"text\":\"*   **Walking:** Be mindful not to block the way of pedestrians when walking in groups on narrow streets.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[13,15],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4172,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4097,\"text\":\"*   **Smoking:** Only smoke in designated areas; never smoke while walking.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[17,18],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4277,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4205,\"text\":\"Kyoto has an excellent, yet sometimes crowded, public transport network.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[17,11],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4427,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4279,\"text\":\"*   **Buses:** Considered the **best way to get around** for reaching many central attractions not near train stations (e.g., Kinkaku-ji, Ryoan-ji).\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[17],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4497,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4443,\"text\":\"Enter at the back door and exit/pay at the front door.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[17,11,19],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4668,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4603,\"text\":\"Railways connect to major hubs like Arashiyama and Fushimi Inari.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[17,20],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4795,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4669,\"text\":\"*   **IC Cards:** Using an IC card (like ICOCA, Suica, or PASMO) is the easiest way to pay for virtually all trains and buses.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[19],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":4930,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4796,\"text\":\"*   **Passes:** Consider a **Subway & Bus One-Day Pass** for unlimited travel on both systems if you plan extensive travel in one day.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[11,18],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5101,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":4931,\"text\":\"*   **Walking & Biking:** Kyoto is relatively compact and flat, making walking and cycling great for exploring historic districts (like Gion/Higashiyama) or scenic paths.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[21,22,23],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5252,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5157,\"text\":\"Kyoto is generally considered one of the safest cities in the world, with very low crime rates.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[22,23],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5344,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5254,\"text\":\"*   **General Safety:** You can safely walk the streets at night, even as a solo traveler.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[21,22],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5452,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5345,\"text\":\"Exercise common sense, such as keeping an eye on your belongings in crowded markets or on public transport.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[21,23],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5503,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5453,\"text\":\"*   **Emergency Numbers:**\\n    *   Police: **110**\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[23],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5548,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5508,\"text\":\"*   Medical Emergency/Ambulance: **119**\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[22],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5657,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5549,\"text\":\"*   **Natural Risks:** Be aware that the risk of natural disasters, such as earthquakes, is considered high.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[24],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5819,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5658,\"text\":\"If venturing into mountain areas, check for recent **Asiatic black bear** sightings and take precautions like traveling in groups or carrying noise-making items.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[21],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":5962,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5925,\"text\":\"Routine vaccinations are always wise.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[23,14],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6184,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":5963,\"text\":\"*   **Geiko/Maiko Etiquette:** If you spot a *Geiko* (Geisha) or *Maiko* in districts like Gion, be respectful; do not swarm them or take photos without explicit permission, as they are often on their way to appointments.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[25,10],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6557,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6313,\"text\":\"*   **Day 1 (Arrival & Eastern Kyoto I):** Explore **Kiyomizu-dera** early, walk down the historic **Ninenzaka/Sannenzaka** streets, and perhaps participate in an early evening **tea ceremony** in the Higashiyama area (near Camellia Tea House).\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[5,7,26],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6756,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6558,\"text\":\"*   **Day 2 (Southern Kyoto):** Visit **Fushimi Inari Taisha** for a morning hike through the *torii* gates, then explore the Zen garden and potentially a simple tea service at **Tofuku-ji Temple**.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[6,7],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":6937,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":6831,\"text\":\"Visit **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** and its garden, followed by a contemplative walk through the **Bamboo Grove**.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[9,7,10],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":7202,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":7119,\"text\":\"Consider a casual tea experience near Kinkaku-ji at a venue like Tea Ceremony KOTO.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[2,9,27],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":7448,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":7381,\"text\":\"Explore **Ginkaku-ji** and walk part of the **Philosopher's Path**.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[8,10],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":7627,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":7449,\"text\":\"*   **Day 6 (Central/Zen Deep Dive):** Visit **Nijo Castle** for history, then explore the quieter **Daitoku-ji** complex for Zen gardens and perhaps a meditation/tea experience.\"}},{\"confidence_scores\":null,\"grounding_chunk_indices\":[7],\"segment\":{\"end_index\":7867,\"part_index\":null,\"start_index\":7686,\"text\":\"If time/interest allows, take the train/bus out to **Kurama-dera** (a mountain temple) for hiking and spiritual connection, which can be combined with a visit to Kifune Shrine area.\"}}],\"retrieval_metadata\":null,\"retrieval_queries\":null,\"search_entry_point\":{\"rendered_content\":\"<style>\\n.container {\\n  align-items: center;\\n  border-radius: 8px;\\n  display: flex;\\n  font-family: Google Sans, Roboto, sans-serif;\\n  font-size: 14px;\\n  line-height: 20px;\\n  padding: 8px 12px;\\n}\\n.chip {\\n  display: inline-block;\\n  border: solid 1px;\\n  border-radius: 16px;\\n  min-width: 14px;\\n  padding: 5px 16px;\\n  text-align: center;\\n  user-select: none;\\n  margin: 0 8px;\\n  -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;\\n